# Web security

The always-on security rule wins if this file disagrees with it.

`frontend/next.config.ts` rewrites `/api/v1` to the backend and builds a standalone bundle. It does not define a Content-Security-Policy. Do not paste a second, conflicting header policy into a new file. Image optimization is off and SVG is allowed there for self-hosted cameras. Do not replace that with a remote image host.

## Cookies and transport

- `access_token` and `refresh_token` are httpOnly. Do not make either readable from JavaScript. `refresh_token` is path-scoped to `/api/v1/auth`.
- `COOKIE_SECURE` defaults to true. `COOKIE_SAMESITE` defaults to `lax` because the LAN frontend and the Cloudflare Tunnel hostname proxy `/api/v1` on the same site (Next rewrite or nginx). Set `COOKIE_SAMESITE=none` only when the browser calls the API on a different site, and keep `COOKIE_SECURE=true` in that case. A cleartext HTTP deployment sets `COOKIE_SECURE=false`. Do not force `Secure` on that HTTP path, and do not drop `Secure` on the HTTPS path.
- Cloudflare Tunnel is an intentional remote-access path. Do not treat the tunnel itself as a defect. The tunnel token is a secret and stays on the server.

## XSS, scripts, and forms

- Do not inject unsanitized HTML or assign user HTML to `innerHTML`.
- Do not add a third-party script. Prefer the dependencies the app already ships.
- State-changing calls go through `frontend/lib/api-client.ts` to the API. Validate on the server. The client check is not the security boundary.
- Do not put secrets in the URL, the document, or `localStorage`. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` stays public.
